   "question": "What have MPs said about housing affordability and home ownership?",
   "answer": "MPs made conflicting statements about housing affordability and home ownership.\n\n- **Opposition/Labor MPs argued that there is a housing affordability crisis.** They said low- and middle-income families, as well as young people, are finding it increasingly difficult to buy homes. They cited rising house prices, such as a typical Melbourne house jumping from four years to seven years of average earnings, and noted that housing affordability had fallen to its lowest level in years. They also criticised the government for not attending the debate and for failing to help those who have \u201cmissed the boat,\u201d including public housing tenants and people on low incomes.\n\n- **Government/Coalition MPs rejected the idea of a crisis.** They said home ownership had \u201cnever been as achievable\u201d and that housing affordability was stronger under the coalition than under Labor. They pointed to lower interest rates\u20146.55 per cent compared with 17 per cent under Labor\u2014and the First Home Owners Grant, which they said helped hundreds of thousands of Australians enter the housing market. They also argued that rising house prices increased the wealth of most homeowners and announced measures such as the Productivity Commission inquiry and the Prime Minister\u2019s task force on home ownership.\n\n- **MPs also debated specific policies.** Some MPs criticised the GST for increasing housing costs and hitting public housing tenants, while others defended the government\u2019s economic management. On mortgage brokers, some MPs called for national regulation to protect home buyers, while government MPs said the government was assisting states to develop a uniform approach.",
