   "question": "What positions have been taken on negative gearing and property tax concessions?",
   "answer": "Based on the provided context, the positions taken are:\n\n**On negative gearing:**\n- The government/coalition said: \u201cWe on this side of the House will not be abolishing negative gearing, because we believe that if you make a loss on an investment you should be able to set that loss against income, as you can under the normal principles of taxation law.\u201d So the government supports keeping negative gearing.\n- The opposition/Labor Party was inconsistent. At one point the Deputy Leader of the Opposition said abolishing negative gearing was \u201ccertainly one option that could be contemplated,\u201d but later issued a statement saying: \u201cWe are not, however, proposing to embrace ACOSS's proposal to end negative gearing.\u201d\n- Later, the member for Werriwa said negative gearing was \u201cpart of Labor's policy review\u201d and that \u201cany smart policy maker would be looking at it,\u201d but the next day the member for Hotham said: \u201cWe are not reviewing negative gearing.\u201d\n\n**On property tax concessions:**\n- The government said it introduced the First Home Owners Scheme, that there is no GST on an established home, and that it would keep up pressure on the states over stamp duties.\n- The government said state Labor governments impose stamp duty, and that since 1996 stamp duty on an average home had risen to $16,000 from $6,000 in both Melbourne and Sydney, while the opposition was silent on stamp duty.\n- The member for Werriwa was described as supporting higher taxation, including extending capital gains tax to the family home and introducing a local betterment tax.",
