   "question": "What has parliament said about social and public housing supply?",
   "answer": "Based on the context, parliament discussed several aspects of public and social housing supply:\n\n- Public housing is described as a **state responsibility**, with the Commonwealth providing funding through the Commonwealth-State Housing Agreement (CSHA) and rent assistance, but not administering waiting lists or maintenance.\n- Some speakers **criticised cuts** to public housing funding, including a $200 million cut in the 1997\u201398 budget, further CSHA reductions of $50 million per year, and warnings that GST-related impacts could cut more than $100 million from public housing budgets and result in fewer houses being built.\n- **Waiting lists were said to be growing**: in Victoria, from 30,076 to 53,379 people between 1987 and 1997; nationally, about 12% of applicants waited more than five years; an older person in Victoria could wait up to ten years; one constituent was told she could wait another three years after already waiting 16 months.\n- The government responded that Commonwealth outlays for public housing plus rent assistance had increased by **$232 million in real terms** since 1997\u201398, with an extra **$75 million over four years** for Indigenous housing, and that states also received GST revenue.\n- Parliament also considered and approved **specific housing supply projects**, mostly for Defence Housing Authority developments: 72 family houses at Adamstown, Newcastle, at $21.6 million; 50 mixed residential dwellings at Stirling, ACT, at $11.5 million; off-base housing at Queanbeyan; housing redevelopment at Enoggera; and a housing development at Parap Grove, Darwin.",
